# Summary
Introduced UI/consent regression: the commit removed visible controls for security/privacy-sensitive auto camera behavior without clearing or ignoring persisted values.
The frontend now hides the `openCameraBeforeBatch` and `closeCameraAfterBatch` controls from the auto-capture UI, but it only defaults them to false when they are undefined. If a user already had these fields set to true, or opens an attacker-supplied JSON config containing true values, the settings remain active with no normal UI control to review or disable them. The backend still uses these values to send OSC commands that open the VRChat camera, enable stream camera mode/streaming, and close the camera after capture. Combined with scheduled auto-capture and Discord auto-post settings, this can cause unexpected capture/posting behavior after a user saves an imported or stale config.

# Validation
## Rubric
- [x] Confirm the commit/current frontend removed visible controls or v-model bindings for `openCameraBeforeBatch` and `closeCameraAfterBatch`.
- [x] Confirm frontend defaulting only applies when the fields are undefined, preserving existing true values.
- [x] Confirm imported/stale JSON config can enter the settings flow and be saved without clearing these hidden fields.
- [x] Confirm backend config normalization/defaults do not forcibly clear persisted true values.
- [x] Dynamically confirm backend auto-capture still honors true values by sending OSC camera open/stream/close commands.
## Report
Validated the suspected UI/consent regression. Targeted frontend check showed there are no remaining normal UI bindings for the two sensitive flags: `autoCaptureSettings.capture.openCameraBeforeBatch: 0`, `autoCaptureSettings.capture.closeCameraAfterBatch: 0`, and the old Japanese labels are absent. The only frontend references are `src/frontend/src/main.js:206-207`, which set defaults to false only when the fields are undefined, so existing/imported true values are preserved. The JSON import path is reachable via drag/drop: `src/frontend/src/main.js:737-742` calls `api.OpenSettings(jsonPaths[0])`, and saving uses the unmodified `state.config` via `api.SaveConfig` at `src/frontend/src/main.js:1634-1657`. Backend config normalization defines the fields at `src/internal/appcore/config.go:140-141` and defaults them false at `src/internal/appcore/config.go:371-372`, but `Normalize()` does not force persisted true values back to false. Backend execution still honors the flags: `src/internal/appcore/autocapture.go:269-299` sends `/usercamera/Mode`, `/usercamera/SmoothMovement`, and `/usercamera/Streaming=true` when `OpenCameraBeforeBatch` is true; `src/internal/appcore/autocapture.go:339-348` sends `/usercamera/Streaming=false` and `/usercamera/Close` when `CloseCameraAfterBatch` is true; `src/internal/appcore/autocapture.go:897-912` refreshes stream camera per shot when the open flag is true. I added a temporary targeted Go PoC test that wrote an attacker/stale JSON config with both flags true, loaded it with `LoadConfig`, ran one auto-capture against a local UDP OSC listener, and asserted the side effects. The test passed and logged OSC packets including `/usercamera/Mode` int 2, `/usercamera/Streaming` true, `/usercamera/Streaming` false, and `/usercamera/Close` true. Crash validation was not applicable because this is a logic/privacy regression; a debug test binary was built and run successfully. Valgrind and gdb were attempted but are not installed in the container.

